  "body": "What happens: the analytic wire contour of #3937 (`crates/motion/src/wire_edm/arc_cut.rs`, `stitch`) joins two arcs whose snapped ends differ by a few nanometres with a connector line. The post rounds that line to the coordinates of the arc end, so the program has a `G1` block with no motion after the arc.\n\nEvidence: the golden `crates/app/tests/golden/nc/wire-iso/wire_dxf_spline_die.nc` of PR #3958 has 74 `G3` blocks and 72 zero-length `G1` blocks. A DXF SPLINE or ELLIPSE (#1865) is read as tangent arcs, so each joint of such a chain is an arc-arc joint.\n\nExpected: two tangent arcs follow each other with no connector block. The program is about half as long.\n\nWhy it matters: the blocks are safe (the laws check each move), but they double the program length of each curve, and some controls warn on a block with no motion.\n\nDone means: a spline die posts with no `G1` block between two arcs, the kerf and cover laws still pass, and the margin note still covers the nanometre offsets. The goldens of #3937 stay byte-identical, or the PR names each one that moves.\n\nSuggested labels (I cannot set labels): `P7`, `effort:high` (posted G-code, safety floor), `area:wire`, `source:agent`.\n",
